  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1142 AM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nDRY MONDAY:\n\nQuiet weather and seasonable temperatures are expected today.\n\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES RETURN:\n\nOn Tuesday a shortwave trough will track over the region. As it\napproaches our area increased southwest flow will advect \nmoisture back into our area. PWATs appear to be 180-200% of \nnormal for a good portion of the CWA, with the highest values \nforecast to be south of I-70 especially near the Four Corners. \nGiven this moisture and forcing from the wave scattered to \nwidespread showers and storms are expected. Instability and \nshear from the system will support the potential for a few \nstronger storms. Also, there could be localized heavy rainfall \nin the afternoon. Showers may linger Tuesday night as the wave \ntracks over Wyoming and drags a weak cold front through. On \nWednesday the moisture becomes confined to the southeast \nportion of the area, so this may allow for the northwest to dry \nout. Models are hinting at a subtle wave moving in from New \nMexico on Wednesday into Thursday. It would provide lift and \nreinforce the moisture as well therefore precipitation chances \nincrease once again. The highest QPF for this round is focusing \naround the southern San Juans and southern valleys. In fact, \nthere are indications some spots could get several inches, which\nincreases the potential for flooding. Rainfall is possible in \nthe central and northern mountains, but in general the amounts\nas lower. Uncertainty increases on whether the moisture gets \npushed out of the area or leaves enough to support showers on \nThursday and Friday. Either way it appears that moisture will \nnot go far so it will not take much to return.",
